<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Buchau</h1><p class="st-meta">1347 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Bad Buchau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A house of noble canonesses on the Federsee whose princess-abbesses ruled a small territory of the Holy Roman Empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tradition credits the founding of the abbey around 770 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelindis-of-buchau">Adelindis</a>, a noblewoman linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a>, on an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federsee">Federsee</a> in upper Swabia. It became a house of secular canonesses drawn only from the high nobility. Its status under the emperor was confirmed in the 14th century, and its abbess came to rank as a princess of the empire.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The princess-abbess sat on the Swabian bench of prelates in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a> and governed villages around the lake and estates elsewhere. The canonesses took no permanent vows and could leave to marry. The neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-buchau">Free Imperial City of Buchau</a> quarrelled with the abbey over rights for centuries. The house remained Catholic.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1803 the abbey was secularised and, with the town, granted to the princes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-thurn-and-taxis">Thurn und Taxis</a> as compensation for lost lands. In 1806 the territory passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg</a>. The abbey church of Saints Cornelius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprian">Cyprian</a> survives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-buchau">Bad Buchau</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="prince bishopric, 1347 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 82 V46 Q50 12 70 46 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 30 V82 M40 58 H60"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">prince bishopric</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:86.51%;width:9.07%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1347 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bad Buchau</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Buchau Abbey, Stift Buchau</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1575&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-buchau" aria-label="Imperial Abbey of Buchau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4904 495 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Abbey of Buchau lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5224 751L5229 694M5224 751L5279 723"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5279 723h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5224 751h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5224 751h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1575&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-buchau">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
